{"issue":{"title":"Add padLeft to strutil for zero-padded invoice numbers, and make the README list every exported function","problem":"index.js exports capitalize, slugify and truncate. Nothing pads a value on the left, so an invoice number like 42 cannot be turned into 00042 with strutil. The README is supposed to list the functions, but it is already incomplete: it has sections for capitalize(s) and truncate(s, n) and none for slugify, which index.js exports and test/strutil.test.js tests.","reason":"The invoice service needs fixed-width, zero-padded invoice numbers. People use the README to find helpers, so an export it leaves out (slugify today, and padLeft once it is added) is effectively hidden.","outcome":"strutil exports a padLeft function that left-pads a string or number to a given width, padding with zeros by default. It is covered by the npm test suite, and the README has a section for every function index.js exports, including slugify and padLeft.","expect":["index.js exports a function named padLeft, and padLeft(42, 5) returns the string '00042'","padLeft('42', 5) returns '00042', and padLeft('7', 3, ' ') returns '  7' (the third argument is the fill character)","padLeft returns a value that is already as wide as the width, or wider, unchanged as a string: padLeft('123456', 5) returns '123456' and padLeft(12345, 5) returns '12345'","test/strutil.test.js contains at least one test that calls padLeft, and `npm test` passes with 0 failures","README.md has a `## name(` heading for every function exported from index.js, including padLeft and slugify"],"proofs":[{"line":"index.js exports a function named padLeft, and padLeft(42, 5) returns the string '00042'","proof":"run","how":"node --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft} from './index.js'; const r = padLeft(42, 5); if (typeof padLeft !== 'function' || r !== '00042') { console.error(JSON.stringify(r)); process.exit(1) }\""},{"line":"padLeft('42', 5) returns '00042', and padLeft('7', 3, ' ') returns '  7' (the third argument is the fill character)","proof":"run","how":"node --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft} from './index.js'; const a = padLeft('42', 5), b = padLeft('7', 3, ' '); if (a !== '00042' || b !== '  7') { console.error(JSON.stringify([a, b])); process.exit(1) }\""},{"line":"padLeft returns a value that is already as wide as the width, or wider, unchanged as a string: padLeft('123456', 5) returns '123456' and padLeft(12345, 5) returns '12345'","proof":"run","how":"node --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft} from './index.js'; const a = padLeft('123456', 5), b = padLeft(12345, 5); if (a !== '123456' || b !== '12345') { console.error(JSON.stringify([a, b])); process.exit(1) }\""},{"line":"test/strutil.test.js contains at least one test that calls padLeft, and `npm test` passes with 0 failures","proof":"run","how":"grep -q 'padLeft(' test/strutil.test.js && npm test"},{"line":"README.md has a `## name(` heading for every function exported from index.js, including padLeft and slugify","proof":"run","how":"node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; import fs from 'node:fs'; const r = fs.readFileSync('README.md', 'utf8'); const names = Object.keys(m).filter(k => typeof m[k] === 'function'); const missing = names.filter(n => !new RegExp('^## ' + n + '\\\\(', 'm').test(r)); if (!names.includes('padLeft') || !names.includes('slugify') || missing.length) { console.error('missing:', missing, 'exports:', names); process.exit(1) }\""}],"non_goals":["Changing how capitalize, slugify or truncate behave","A padRight function or any other new helper","Publishing a new npm release or bumping the package version","Adding CI configuration"],"assumptions":["The request only says 'padLeft' and gives the example 42 -> 00042, so the signature is open. I assumed padLeft(value, width, fill = '0'): it accepts a string or a number, returns a string, pads with '0' by default, and takes an optional single-character fill as the third argument.","I assumed padLeft never truncates: a value already as wide as the width, or wider, comes back unchanged as a string.","'Keep the README's list of functions complete' means every function exported from index.js has its own '## name(args)' section, following the README's existing style. That includes the slugify section the README is already missing.","How padLeft is implemented is left to the builder (String.prototype.padStart is one option). The request does not require any particular approach.","Behaviour for a negative or non-integer width, or for a fill longer than one character, is not specified, and the checks do not test it."]}}